research/audit/wave9-refuter-tasks/ex-thomae-values-and-oscillation-computed.md-217-      "claim": "Claim 2: let $q \\ge 1$ be a natural and put $x := 1/\\iota(q)$. Then $\\iota(q)x = 1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, so $q \\in Q(x)$ and $q(x) \\le q$. Conversely, if $1 \\le k \\le q$ is a natural with $\\iota(k)x = \\iota(k)/\\iota(q) \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, then $k < q$ would put $\\iota(k)/\\iota(q)$ strictly between $0$ and $1$, which no integer is; so $k = q$. Hence $q(x) = q$ and $t(1/\\iota(q)) = 1/\\iota(q)$. Taking $q = 2$ gives $t(1/2) = 1/2$. [A1, L2]",
research/audit/wave9-refuter-tasks/ex-thomae-values-and-oscillation-computed.md-218-      "step": "1.2",
