research/audit/wave9-dispatch/certifier-a6r2-thm-baire-one-continuity-points.log-3019-research/audit/wave9-dispatch/certifier-a6r2-thm-baire-one-continuity-points.prompt.md:179:- **The conclusion is sharp in the sense that "meager" cannot be improved to "at most countable".** The theorem constrains the discontinuity set by category, not by cardinality. Nothing above bounds the size of $D$; a meager set can be uncountable, the Cantor set being one ([[thm-cantor-set-properties]]), so the theorem leaves open how large a discontinuity set a Baire class one function may have. What it does exclude outright is a Baire class one function on $[0,1]$ that is nowhere continuous, and the companion page spends exactly that on the Dirichlet function.
