research/audit/wave9-dispatch/certifier-a6r2-thm-baire-one-continuity-points.log-4644-[L1] $f$ is continuous at $c$ exactly when for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$ with $|f(x) - f(c)| < \varepsilon$ for every $x \in A$ with $|x - c| < \delta$; equivalently for every $x \in A \cap N_\delta(c)$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
