research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1912-## Remarks
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1913-
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md:1914:- **Where the hypothesis of Baire class one is used.** The hypothesis enters through the approximating sequence fixed in step 1.1. Pointwise convergence is used in steps 2.2 and 4.1, and continuity of the approximants is used in steps 2.1 and 4.2. These facts establish the refinement claim in step 6.1. From step 7.1 onward the proof uses only that claim, oscillation, and category.
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1915-
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1916-- **The conclusion is sharp in the sense that "meager" cannot be improved to "at most countable".** The theorem constrains the discontinuity set by category, not by cardinality. Nothing above bounds the size of $D$; a meager set can be uncountable, the Cantor set being one ([[thm-cantor-set-properties]]), so the theorem leaves open how large a discontinuity set a Baire class one function may have. What it does exclude outright is a Baire class one function on $[0,1]$ that is nowhere continuous, and the companion page spends exactly that on the Dirichlet function.
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1917-
research/audit/wave9-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-thm-discontinuity-set-is-f-sigma.prompt.md-1918-- **The dense set is not claimed to be uncountable, and no measure statement is made.** Meagerness is a statement about category alone ([[def-nowhere-dense-meager]]); nothing above bears on the measure of $D$, and the two notions of smallness are independent, as [[cor-q-is-meager-and-not-g-delta]] and the fat Cantor set already record.
